# Attempt 3: Choosing Arch, and Getting the ThinkPad

## Why I was still looking for a new distro

As mentioned in the README, I'd originally planned to continue dual
booting, starting fresh rather than sticking with Fedora, since an
unfamiliar environment is where I learn the most. I wanted something
similar to Fedora, since to me it was the perfect mix of Windows and Linux:
simple, but letting the user customize their system without much
restriction. It also needed to stay up to date with a frequent release
cycle and have access to the latest open-source software, and it needed
solid NVIDIA driver support, which Fedora had.

## Distros considered

**Arch (via Omarchy):** From Omarchy, I'd already learned about Arch. On
paper, it felt like the perfect fit. It lets the user pick and choose
exactly what they want in their system, rather than holding their hand and
installing everything for them. But after more research, I realised arch
wasn't quite the perfect fit after all, since its NVIDIA support, while
present, wasn't as good as the proprietary drivers Fedora offered.

**Mint and Ubuntu:** As mentioned in previous attempts, I'd already
experienced Ubuntu through university. It felt too similar to Windows, and
I wanted something new rather than going back to something I'd already
used. I'd classify Mint as Ubuntu-like too, which is why I grouped them
together. That's why neither was chosen, even though they're some of the
most popular distros with excellent, wide package support.

**Fedora, but GNOME this time:** Before anything else, I considered going
back to Fedora, just downloading the GNOME version instead of the KDE
Plasma version I'd originally picked, believing it to be more customizable.
After digging through videos and blogs, I found ricing a GNOME environment
easier than KDE, and that was enough to make me consider going back. I
ultimately decided against it, since it would still be Fedora underneath,
just with a different desktop environment, and I wanted a genuinely new
experience. That said, GNOME piqued my interest, and I wanted a distro with
GNOME as its primary desktop environment.

**Gentoo:** Since my main laptop, the Nitro V15, is a gaming laptop, I
thought Gentoo might be a good pick for it. But it has the same kind of
simple design philosophy I was already looking for in Fedora, and it's
another Arch variant, which made me want to go for the original Arch
instead.

**Debian:** Debian is popular and stable, but its long update cycle means
the distro falls out of date quickly. That makes it a good fit for a
homelab or server, but not for a workstation, where I'd actually want
newer packages day to day. So I ruled it out.

**Pop!_OS (the original plan):** Out of everything I looked at, I landed on
Pop!_OS. It was simple and clean like Fedora, with GNOME available as a
desktop option. I looked into it a little and liked what I saw, both the
design and the overall feel, and planned to look into it further once I'd
finished the rest of the repo, then properly document the install and the
thinking behind it.

## Getting the ThinkPad X230

While researching the distro, I was also working on assignments for my next
module, building this repo, and searching Amazon, eBay, and Facebook
Marketplace for a cheap secondhand laptop. Eventually, I came across a
listing for a ThinkPad X230 for £50 (originally £95, reduced). I contacted
the seller and got a quick response.

Checking the specs, it looked like a great deal. Lenovo ThinkPads,
especially older generations, are known for being built to last, and this
one still had room for upgrades (extra RAM slots, keyboard replacement, and
so on). I spoke with the seller for a couple of hours. I didn't try to
bargain the price down further, since I already thought it was a good deal,
especially with the 8 GB of RAM it came with. He mentioned other people were
interested in viewing it too, and since I really wanted it, I sent a £25
deposit to hold it, and told him I'd come on Thursday to view it in person
and buy it if it checked out.

I didn't want to arrange delivery, since it's too easy to fake listings and
records that way, and I wanted to see the laptop in person to reduce the
chance of a scam. For the deposit, he gave me a friend's bank details rather
than his own, which raised a flag, but I went ahead anyway, since bank
transfers are relatively safe. To build trust, he sent me a photo of his
driver's licence, though I stayed cautious regardless. I'd originally
planned to bring a friend to the pickup, but that didn't work out, so I went
alone. Before the meetup, he asked if I could just bring cash, which was
another odd request, but I agreed anyway. To be safe, I did a quick
background check through his Facebook profile for anything suggesting a
prior scam or criminal history, and found nothing concerning, so I decided
it was safe enough to go ahead.

On Thursday, I took a two-hour train journey to the pickup spot with the
remaining £25 in cash. When I arrived, he handed over the laptop and told me
he'd reset it, but it clearly hadn't been reset properly, since it was still
sitting on the network setup screen of the Windows out-of-box experience.
That raised more red flags, and I considered asking for my deposit back and
walking away. But the screen looked clean, with no dead pixels, so I decided
to take the gamble and buy it.

Once home, I started testing it immediately. It was extremely dirty, but I
ran all my checks before cleaning it, so I wouldn't waste time. I first
completed the Windows reset properly myself, without signing into my
Microsoft account, in case the previous owner had left anything behind that
could access my information.

I opened the terminal and checked the SSD's health with:
```powershell
Get-PhysicalDisk | Select FriendlyName, HealthStatus, OperationalStatus
```
which reported it as healthy. I then checked Settings to confirm the specs
matched the listing, which they did. I checked battery health with:
```powershell
powercfg /batteryhealth
```
which reported it at over 60% capacity, so I didn't need to replace it
right away.

With everything checking out, I cleaned the laptop, and it looked great
afterward. The one issue was dust built up behind the keyboard, which I
didn't know how to remove properly, so I used a blow dryer to clear it out.
It worked reasonably well, and I ended up with a ThinkPad in genuinely good
shape, worth a lot more than what I paid for it.

## Why Arch, in the end

With a new laptop in hand, I decided to put Linux on it. Arch has a strong
reputation for reviving old laptops and making them feel new again, and
after every other distro I'd considered got ruled out one way or another, I
found myself wanting to try Arch more and more. That night, I abandoned the
Pop!_OS plan and started downloading Arch instead. After a couple of days of
daily driving it, I haven't run into any major problems, though I did hit a
few difficulties during the installation itself, covered in the problems
section for this attempt.
